1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Transcriber: Göksu Doğan
Reviewer: Vaia Katsarou

2
00:00:10,941 --> 00:00:15,078
I first found my love of engineering
and automotive technology

3
00:00:15,078 --> 00:00:17,447
a mere six miles down the road.

4
00:00:18,248 --> 00:00:21,385
With my engineering degree
I designed steering columns,

5
00:00:21,385 --> 00:00:23,687
I launched seeding programs,

6
00:00:24,021 --> 00:00:26,356
I went back to school again
 and became a lawyer,

7
00:00:26,356 --> 00:00:31,194
where now I work with automotive companies
to get technology into the market.

8
00:00:32,162 --> 00:00:33,964
It's a fantastic opportunity.

9
00:00:33,964 --> 00:00:38,268
But what really set me aflame
 was driverless cars.

10
00:00:38,702 --> 00:00:42,739
The notion of these sensors
and these systems, the AI,

11
00:00:42,973 --> 00:00:45,342
just fascinating technology.

12
00:00:45,742 --> 00:00:47,711
But what, really, drew me

13
00:00:48,545 --> 00:00:52,049
was the vision of a more
 perfect driving future,

14
00:00:52,215 --> 00:00:55,419
inclusion for the disabled,
for the elderly,

15
00:00:55,552 --> 00:00:58,188
and for those underserved communities.

16
00:00:58,622 --> 00:01:00,624
To me that was important.

17
00:01:00,724 --> 00:01:04,161
And what cemented this
 was my very first speaking gig

18
00:01:04,161 --> 00:01:05,963
on autonomous vehicles.

19
00:01:06,296 --> 00:01:08,899
Now, granted, I was there
speaking about what happens

20
00:01:08,899 --> 00:01:12,002
when these car crashes happen
 and who’s to blame.

21
00:01:12,269 --> 00:01:15,072
And everybody wanted to know
 who do you blame.

22
00:01:15,072 --> 00:01:20,043
But there were people at that conference
that, really, were doing amazing work.

23
00:01:20,243 --> 00:01:22,980
I met the delegation
from the State of Nevada,

24
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:26,450
and they told me about Sam Schmidt,

25
00:01:27,050 --> 00:01:31,221
a race car driver, who was rendered
 paralyzed following an accident.

26
00:01:31,822 --> 00:01:37,928
It was Sam's goal to get back behind
the proverbial wheel of a vehicle again,

27
00:01:38,528 --> 00:01:41,565
and they wanted
 that inclusive future for him.

28
00:01:42,666 --> 00:01:47,137
In 2016, Sam was blessed to be one
of the first people in the country

29
00:01:47,571 --> 00:01:50,140
with a license to ride.

30
00:01:50,374 --> 00:01:53,343
Now, he did have his opportunity
and he’s there,

31
00:01:53,343 --> 00:01:55,979
but what’s happened to technology since.

32
00:01:56,513 --> 00:01:58,348
The cars keep getting smarter,

33
00:01:58,348 --> 00:02:00,517
I mean, they’re computers on wheels,

34
00:02:00,651 --> 00:02:03,420
but we still have challenges.

35
00:02:04,087 --> 00:02:05,822
Let's take a look at an example.

36
00:02:06,256 --> 00:02:08,091
The Tesla full self drive.

37
00:02:08,392 --> 00:02:12,262
Now, yes, Tesla full self drive
 is not an automated vehicle,

38
00:02:12,262 --> 00:02:13,530
not, not really.

39
00:02:13,730 --> 00:02:15,298
But let’s look at how it works

40
00:02:15,298 --> 00:02:18,835
because that’s really critical
to this whole operation of AVs.

41
00:02:20,103 --> 00:02:22,606
Drivers collect all of this information,

42
00:02:22,606 --> 00:02:24,207
Tesla synthesizes it,

43
00:02:24,207 --> 00:02:27,310
they code it and put it back
into the AI engine,

44
00:02:27,310 --> 00:02:29,880
and the car learns to drive and improves.

45
00:02:30,714 --> 00:02:32,049
But wait a minute.

46
00:02:32,249 --> 00:02:35,218
What is your average
Tesla driver look like?

47
00:02:35,952 --> 00:02:42,159
Well, demographics tell us they have,
at least, an income of $150,000,

48
00:02:42,426 --> 00:02:44,861
75% of them are male,

49
00:02:45,262 --> 00:02:48,398
70% of them don’t have children at home,

50
00:02:49,599 --> 00:02:51,735
and over 80% are white.

51
00:02:52,436 --> 00:02:55,172
Now, LendingTree came out
with some recent statistics

52
00:02:55,172 --> 00:02:57,674
that show us that there are
 a couple of other things

53
00:02:57,674 --> 00:02:59,276
that we may want to think about.

54
00:02:59,276 --> 00:03:01,078
According to LendingTree,

55
00:03:01,078 --> 00:03:05,282
they are the second most likely
 to have vehicle incidents

56
00:03:05,716 --> 00:03:09,820
and the most likely to have
 vehicle crashes per thousand vehicles.

57
00:03:10,921 --> 00:03:13,056
Now, not to be too harsh on Tesla,

58
00:03:13,356 --> 00:03:15,592
there are shortcomings in the data sets

59
00:03:15,592 --> 00:03:18,428
and the collection
of other automakers as well.

60
00:03:19,196 --> 00:03:23,033
And when you have shortcomings,
when you have limited data sets,

61
00:03:23,667 --> 00:03:26,169
unfortunate situations happen.

62
00:03:26,870 --> 00:03:33,343
In 2019, Georgia Tech conducted
 a seminal study on these vision systems

63
00:03:33,343 --> 00:03:35,512
and how they detect pedestrians.

64
00:03:36,213 --> 00:03:40,751
Georgia Tech found 
that there was, nearly, a 10% difference

65
00:03:40,751 --> 00:03:43,620
in the ability of these systems to detect

66
00:03:44,387 --> 00:03:47,390
dark skinned people
versus light skinned people.

67
00:03:48,959 --> 00:03:53,530
In 2023, this study was redone 
by King’s College in London.

68
00:03:53,897 --> 00:03:57,234
Now, what King's College
found is things have improved.

69
00:03:57,567 --> 00:04:02,539
7% difference, but King’s College,
also, found other issues.

70
00:04:03,373 --> 00:04:07,477
These systems are 20% more likely
 to detect adults

71
00:04:07,477 --> 00:04:09,045
when compared to children.

72
00:04:10,313 --> 00:04:13,984
Clearly, to get good AV systems
on the road,

73
00:04:13,984 --> 00:04:17,921
we need an inclusive design.

74
00:04:18,755 --> 00:04:21,291
And by inclusive design,
I think of my friends

75
00:04:21,358 --> 00:04:22,492
in the privacy realm,

76
00:04:22,492 --> 00:04:25,595
they wanted to bring privacy to us
 throughout the chain,

77
00:04:25,595 --> 00:04:27,964
they created privacy by design.

78
00:04:27,964 --> 00:04:30,367
So privacy wasn't an afterthought.

79
00:04:30,901 --> 00:04:35,739
To have an inclusive mobility,
we need inclusion by design,

80
00:04:35,772 --> 00:04:37,140
not as an afterthought.

81
00:04:37,607 --> 00:04:39,376
So, how do we get there?

82
00:04:39,810 --> 00:04:42,979
I’ll give you some tenets
of inclusion by design in AI,

83
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:44,981
with three stories.

84
00:04:44,981 --> 00:04:47,617
I’m going to talk about 
a wheelchair and a duck,

85
00:04:47,717 --> 00:04:50,921
honking horns and a taco truck.

86
00:04:51,655 --> 00:04:55,325
So, let’s start in 2016
with the wheelchair and the duck.

87
00:04:58,061 --> 00:05:01,665
Google had this vehicle
and it was testing around the town,

88
00:05:01,665 --> 00:05:05,001
and what it found was
 a wheelchair and a duck.

89
00:05:05,435 --> 00:05:09,706
They called it the renegade granny
 on an electric vehicle

90
00:05:09,706 --> 00:05:11,508
chasing ducks around.

91
00:05:12,242 --> 00:05:14,911
What was fantastic is the vehicle stopped.

92
00:05:15,011 --> 00:05:17,414
It did exactly what it was supposed to do.

93
00:05:17,514 --> 00:05:22,953
It recognized that the electric wheelchair
 had a person on it,

94
00:05:22,953 --> 00:05:24,988
and that the duck was important as well.

95
00:05:24,988 --> 00:05:27,924
So it was able to identify all people.

96
00:05:27,924 --> 00:05:30,694
Expanding upon that, we need systems,

97
00:05:30,694 --> 00:05:34,631
AI, data sets that can identify all people

98
00:05:34,631 --> 00:05:38,668
regardless of their gender, 
their age, or their race.

99
00:05:39,502 --> 00:05:42,339
So let's fast forward
to just a few weeks ago,

100
00:05:42,772 --> 00:05:45,942
Waymo driverless vehicles
in San Francisco.

101
00:05:46,776 --> 00:05:49,546
When a driverless vehicle is done,
when a Waymo is done,

102
00:05:49,546 --> 00:05:52,015
it kind of congregates in a parking lot,

103
00:05:52,382 --> 00:05:55,752
and really it’s an elegant dance
 if you see the videos.

104
00:05:56,653 --> 00:06:00,757
The problem is, when they do this dance,
they get really close to each other,

105
00:06:00,891 --> 00:06:06,963
and they start to honk all night long,

106
00:06:07,430 --> 00:06:10,033
really affecting
those neighborhood communities,

107
00:06:10,033 --> 00:06:14,137
those apartment buildings that are really
 adjacent to that parking lot.

108
00:06:14,738 --> 00:06:16,273
Look at the bigger picture.

109
00:06:16,406 --> 00:06:18,708
That was an unintended consequence.

110
00:06:18,775 --> 00:06:22,279
We need to look at the communities
that are impacted.

111
00:06:22,312 --> 00:06:24,681
Even if the vision system
 isn’t seeing them,

112
00:06:24,681 --> 00:06:28,518
we need to include the larger scale
 and the larger scope.

113
00:06:29,119 --> 00:06:31,421
Let's stay in San Francisco

114
00:06:31,421 --> 00:06:33,757
and rewind back to 2017.

115
00:06:33,857 --> 00:06:35,492
The taco truck.

116
00:06:35,492 --> 00:06:39,062
A vehicle detected a taco truck
 and it stopped.

117
00:06:39,062 --> 00:06:42,032
I mean, come on, who wouldn’t want
to stop for a taco truck?

118
00:06:42,299 --> 00:06:44,501
The problem was,
it didn't start back up again.

119
00:06:44,501 --> 00:06:46,636
It saw the pedestrians
 going back and forth,

120
00:06:46,636 --> 00:06:49,439
and it thought I’ve got to stop,
 I don’t know what to do.

121
00:06:49,439 --> 00:06:52,742
The safety driver took control
and escorted the vehicle away.

122
00:06:53,376 --> 00:06:58,748
Inclusion by design necessitates,
thinking about location and where we’re at

123
00:06:59,249 --> 00:07:01,184
Τhe communities that surround them,

124
00:07:01,184 --> 00:07:04,321
and the interactions,
whether it’s suburban or urban.

125
00:07:05,755 --> 00:07:06,923
So we've got

126
00:07:08,291 --> 00:07:10,260
inclusion by design.

127
00:07:10,260 --> 00:07:12,228
We have identify all people.

128
00:07:12,228 --> 00:07:16,066
We have look at the bigger picture,
and we have think about location.

129
00:07:16,466 --> 00:07:20,737
So, with inclusion by design,
 and remembering the story

130
00:07:20,870 --> 00:07:25,075
of the wheelchair and the duck,  
honking cars and the taco truck

131
00:07:25,075 --> 00:07:29,479
will be able to move towards
 that total inclusion by design.

132
00:07:29,713 --> 00:07:32,582
It’s something we need to teach
 in our engineering schools.

133
00:07:32,582 --> 00:07:36,386
It's something that needs to be considered
by our standards bodies.

134
00:07:36,519 --> 00:07:39,689
It's something that the regulators
need to start thinking,

135
00:07:39,689 --> 00:07:46,629
so,we can, truly, move forward
to that vision of an inclusive mobility.

136
00:07:46,997 --> 00:07:50,400
(Applause)

